{
  "dom_id": "questionContainer",
  "name": "Question 42",
  "problem": "Given that $f(x)=|x-4|+2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nI. $x\\in (-\\infty ,3)$\n\nII. $x\\in (1,\\infty )$\n\nIII. $x\\in (-\\infty ,5]$",
  "worked_solution": "The graph of the function $f(x)=|x-4|+2$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n1\\. Take the curve $y=|x|.$\n\n2\\. Translate it by $4$ units to the right,  to get $y=|x-4|.$\n\n3\\. Finally, translate $y=|x-4|$ by $2$ units up to get $f(x)=|x-4|+2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196632/assets/56956d0f23ae6828098507712fd00525dc4f114a195716dc16e63d8475e9c143.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,3).$  Indeed, on this interval, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196632/assets/deb229df9f445a87215ff7c8fbb796113990df0c15124d5a6a01e199480620a4.png)\n\n   \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on any of the intervals $x\\in (1,\\infty )$  and $(-\\infty ,5].$ On each of these intervals, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196632/assets/eec9da919298bc8576580a0c7461e89625e1f4dbb49b9a5081dc959ca99153df.png)\n\n   \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\".",
